  "body": "Follow-up from #690 / PR #694 (review note).\n\nMost mill generators start their `PathBuilder` cursor at (0, 0, clearance) and open with a rapid to their start point on the clearance plane. That rapid is the program's approach: posts write only a move's end point, so it becomes the `G00` after the tool call.\n\n`PathBuilder::push` drops a zero-length move. When a generator's start point is exactly X0 Y0, the approach rapid therefore disappears. The first posted motion is then whatever follows, typically a plunge or ramp feed from the tool-change position. That is #690's bug, reached through a different door.\n\n#694 closed this for Face by stating a zero-length rapid when the path doesn't open with one. The others are unchecked, including chamfer, the finishing3d family (raster, flat, pencil, polar, morph, flow, shadow, rings, geodesic, deburr) and the 2D ladders.\n\n**Done means:**\n- Each generator that seeds its cursor at (0, 0, clearance) is checked with a fixture whose start is exactly X0 Y0.\n- Any that lose the approach are fixed. A shared rule is preferred, for example a `PathBuilder` or `Toolpath` helper that guarantees the first move is a rapid at clearance, over per-generator patches.\n- A test pins that the first move is a rapid for each affected generator.\n\nRelated: #690, PR #694\n",
